The second thing I wanted to mention was that I'm currently working on my PhD. It's looking at how doctors in prisons can better support people experiencing mental health issues, particularly those who may be suicidal. The research involves interviewing doctors and nurses across England and Wales, and I'm trying to understand what supports are available to help staff manage these situations. We’re also exploring how to improve training and resources so that healthcare professionals feel more equipped when dealing with vulnerable individuals in custody.

We’ve just started this new program where we're bringing in a specialist team from outside the prison system—mental health specialists—to provide additional support to our patients. This includes things like cognitive behavioral therapy and psychological assessments. These specialists come into the prison once a week, but it's still early days. There’s definitely room for improvement, especially in terms of access and continuity of care. But it’s promising because it shows a growing recognition that mental health matters in prisons.

I think it's important to highlight that even though we're in a correctional setting, the duty to protect human rights remains paramount. That means ensuring all prisoners receive appropriate medical attention, regardless of their offense or status within the facility. For instance, someone serving time for a serious crime should still get the same level of care if they need emergency treatment as anyone else. And we're committed to upholding this standard.

Also, I want to emphasize that mental health challenges among incarcerated individuals are widespread—not just limited to those with prior diagnoses. Many people enter prison without known mental health conditions, but the harsh environment can trigger or worsen existing ones. In fact, studies show that suicide rates in prisons are higher than in the general population. So addressing mental health isn’t optional—it’s essential for safety and dignity.

Finally, while we face constraints such as staffing shortages and limited space, we continue to prioritize patient-centered care. Our goal is always to treat each individual with compassion and respect, recognizing their humanity despite their past actions. That commitment guides everything we do.

